---

**Running**

```bash
ansible-playbook -i inventories/sbox/hosts.ini ipa.yml --tags bootstrap --ask-vault-pass
ansible-playbook -i inventories/sbox/hosts.ini ipa.yml
ansible-playbook -i inventories/sbox/hosts.ini ipa.yml --tags hbac
```

Routine runs need no vault password — that's what the keytab buys.

**Set `svc_ansible_principal` to whatever you actually named the account.** The variable carries through every task.

**Confirm idempotency, which is the point of the split**

```bash
ansible-playbook -i inventories/sbox/hosts.ini ipa.yml --tags bootstrap --ask-vault-pass
kvno svc-ansible-kt
ansible-playbook -i inventories/sbox/hosts.ini ipa.yml --tags bootstrap --ask-vault-pass
kvno svc-ansible-kt
```

Same kvno, and the re-key block skipped on the second run. If it fires every time, something still writes the key each bootstrap — check that no `password:` crept back onto the `ipauser` task.

**Possibly droppable**

The `--setattr` sentinel. With no password on the account it may not get stamped at all:

```bash
ipa user-show svc-ansible-kt --all | grep -i expiration
```

If nothing appears, remove that task. If a real date does, keep it.